\description{
Get a single college football team's full-season schedule --
one row per game with opponent, venue, broadcast, score, and result.
}
\details{
Wraps the ESPN site-v2 team schedule endpoint
\code{site.api.espn.com/apis/site/v2/sports/football/college-football/teams/{team_id}/schedule}.
Each row is one scheduled game from the requested team's perspective:
the \verb{opponent_*} columns describe the other team, and \code{team_score} /
\code{opponent_score} / \code{team_winner} describe the outcome (scores are \code{NA}
for games that have not been played). Team ids are ESPN team identifiers
-- enumerate them with \code{\link[=espn_cfb_teams]{espn_cfb_teams()}}.

When \code{team_detail = TRUE} (the default) the ESPN team catalog
(\code{\link[=espn_cfb_teams]{espn_cfb_teams()}}) is fetched once and friendly team fields are joined
onto every team-id column the output carries -- \code{team_id} and
\code{opponent_id}. For each id column \code{X_id} the friendly siblings \code{X_name},
\code{X_abbreviation}, \code{X_location}, \code{X_display_name},
\code{X_short_display_name}, \code{X_nickname}, \code{X_color}, \code{X_alternate_color},
\code{X_logo_href}, and \code{X_logo_dark_href} are inserted immediately after it.
A catalog failure degrades to \code{NA} rather than erroring the wrapper. Set
\code{team_detail = FALSE} to skip the catalog fetch and the join.
}
